---

<LayoutDefault
  image="/images/projects/smart-lawn/grgagrow-visual-sensors.jpg"
  imageAlt="grgaGrow garden monitoring interface with illustrated root zones, soil moisture and temperature readings"
  imageWidth={1265}
  imageHeight={712}
  title="grgaGrow — When does my lawn actually need water? — Gordan Grgić"
  description="Two probes, one ESP32 and the first readings sent to the cloud. A soil-monitoring project for my 49 m² lawn."
>
  <div class="lawn-notes">
    <header class="lawn-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow"><strong>grgaGrow</strong> / soil monitoring</p>
      <h1>When does my lawn actually need water?</h1>
      <p class="lede">I want to see how quickly the soil on my 49 m² lawn dries out in the sun and in the shade.</p>
      <p class="body-copy">
        On the workbench, two probes now share one controller. The first readings have reached the cloud over Wi-Fi.
      </p>
      <ProbeReadingCards />
      <div class="project-actions">
        <a
          class="demo-link live-link"
          href="/demos/my-garden/?public=grgagrow"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer">Follow live readings ↗</a
        >
        <a href="#lawn-hardware">See the bench tests ↓</a>
        <a href="/projects/">← All project notes</a>
      </div>
      <p class="note">
        The readings so far come from bench tests. Measurements from the lawn follow once the probes are installed. Live
        opens without an account.
      </p>
    </header>

    <nav class="section-nav" aria-label="Sections">
      <a href="#lawn-site">In the lawn</a>
      <a href="#lawn-hardware">On the bench</a>
      <a href="#lawn-equipment">Equipment</a>
      <a href="#lawn-software">Garden app</a>
      <a href="#lawn-next">Next steps</a>
    </nav>

    <ProjectOverview
      id="grgagrow"
      items={[
        {
          label: 'Today',
          text: 'Cloud app online; firmware on the ESP32 reads two probes and uploads over Wi-Fi with OTA updates. Calibration and field operation are still to be verified.',
        },
        {
          label: 'My role',
          text: 'Defining the garden workflow and expected behaviour, carrying out bench checks and interpreting the results.',
        },
        {
          label: 'Example',
          text: 'Both probes shipped at the same address. Moving one kept failing until I found that the board never sent the 11-byte write frame.',
        },
        {
          label: 'Next step',
          text: 'Bury both probes in the lawn, check readings in soil, then calibrate pH and moisture before reviewing watering thresholds.',
        },
      ]}
    />

    <section aria-labelledby="lawn-site">
      <p class="folio-label">01 / MEASUREMENTS IN THE LAWN</p>
      <h2 id="lawn-site">Measurements in the lawn.</h2>
      <figure class="lawn-cover">
        <img
          src="/images/projects/smart-lawn/lawn.jpg"
          alt="My 49 square metre lawn with a robotic mower, the future test site for the two soil probes"
          width="1600"
          height="1200"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
          data-group="cover"
        />
        <figcaption>
          THE TEST SITE / My 49 m² lawn. The probes go in next; placement and cable routes must also suit the robotic
          mower.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        This is the part I enjoy: connecting the interface to something physical, making the measurements
        understandable, and checking the whole path from a sensor in the ground to a decision on the screen.
      </p>
      <div class="live-soon">
        <p class="small-label">LIVE READINGS / STARTING SOON</p>
        <p>
          The readings so far come from bench tests. The two probes are going into the lawn now; once they are buried,
          their soil moisture and temperature appear in public Live as they arrive.
        </p>
        <a
          class="demo-link live-link"
          href="/demos/my-garden/?public=grgagrow"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer">Follow the live readings ↗</a
        >
      </div>
      <p>
        The idea behind grgaGrow came from a simple observation: gardens are often watered too much or too little. Water
        is precious, and climate change makes using it thoughtfully even more important. I wanted to explore how a
        small, smart and affordable monitoring system could help people understand what their soil needs and avoid
        unnecessary watering.
      </p>
      <p>
        The system can also be extended with automatic irrigation. Adding a controlled valve and a control module could
        let grgaGrow water the garden based on soil readings and weather information. This is a planned next stage,
        following reliable monitoring and hardware testing.
      </p>
      <p>
        I still water the lawn manually. Sun, shade and soil conditions can make nearby areas behave differently. I want
        to compare how quickly they dry, how the soil temperature changes, and how long water takes to reach a probe.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="lawn-hardware">
      <p class="folio-label">02 / WORK ON THE BENCH</p>
      <h2 id="lawn-hardware">Two probes. One bus. Readings in the cloud.</h2>
      <p>
        The grgaGrow firmware runs on a Waveshare ESP32-S3-RS485-CAN Rev 2.2. It polls a temperature-and-moisture probe
        and a second probe with experimental pH over one RS485 bus, queues the samples and uploads them over Wi-Fi. The
        bench version is complete; the probes are about to go into the lawn.
      </p>
      <dl class="hardware-progress" aria-label="Hardware progress as of 21 November 2026">
        <div><dt>Bench result</dt><dd>Two probes uploading</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Next milestone</dt><dd>Readings from the soil</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Field status</dt><dd>Installation in progress</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <ol class="milestones" aria-label="Milestones from bench to field">
        {
          milestones.map(([date, title, text, state]) => (
            <li class:list={[state]}>
              <time>{date}</time>
              <strong>{title}</strong>
              <small>{text}</small>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>
      <details class="evidence-block" id="lawn-probe-service">
        <summary>Probe Service, my service tool on the Mac</summary>
        <p>Over USB, my Mac shows both probes, the Wi-Fi link, the time and the upload queue on one screen.</p>
        <figure class="service-board">
          <img
            src="/images/projects/smart-lawn/bench/service-board-mock.png"
            alt="The grgaGrow service tool’s Board screen, with mock Wi-Fi, probe and upload status."
            width="1196"
            height="817"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            data-group="grgagrow-service"
            data-index="0"
            data-title="The service tool on the Mac, shown with simulated data."
            tabindex="0"
            role="button"
            aria-label="Enlarge the service tool screenshot with simulated data"
          />
          <figcaption>SIMULATED DATA / The service tool on the Mac, shown with simulated data.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>
          To work on the probes without risking the installed firmware, I built <strong>Probe Service</strong>: a small
          local web app on the Mac, packaged as a Dock app. It loads a bridge image into the ESP32's RAM through the ROM
          bootloader and talks to the probes through it. Nothing is written to flash; a power cycle returns the board to
          its installed firmware.
        </p>
        <dl class="tool-tabs">
          {
            probeServiceTabs.map(([name, text]) => (
              <div>
                <>
                  <dt>{name}</dt>
                  <dd>{text}</dd>
                </>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </dl>
        <div class="engineering-note firmware-example" aria-labelledby="bridge-example-title">
          <p class="small-label">SAFETY LIMIT / BRIDGE IMAGE</p>
          <h3 id="bridge-example-title">Reads are allowed. Writes are locked.</h3>
          <p>
            The bridge forwards only CRC-valid frames to units 1–247: register reads, and one write to the address
            register with a value from 1 to 247. Everything else is answered with DENY.
          </p>
          <p class="note">bridge-image/main/entry.cpp · allowed (excerpt)</p>
          <details class="code-toggle">
            <summary>Show the C++ excerpt</summary>
            <Code
              code={bridgeExample}
              lang="cpp"
              themes={{ light: 'github-light', dark: 'github-dark' }}
              tabindex="0"
              aria-labelledby="bridge-example-title"
              data-firmware-code="true"
            />
          </details>
        </div>
      </details>
      <h3 id="lawn-bench">First connections on the bench.</h3>
      <div
        class="software-gallery bench-gallery"
        aria-label="Photographs from the first grgaGrow bench tests"
        data-gallery-scope
      >
        <figure>
          <img
            src="/images/projects/smart-lawn/bench/bench-setup-front.webp"
